Janis wrote:
> On 12 Okt., 09:23, Philip Potter <p...@see.sig.invalid> wrote:
>> C doesn't constrain the way you think; it only constrains the syntax you
>> write in. If I want to think of a struct obj as an object, I will.
>> Simple OO design can be done using C's features:
>>
>> To make an object, define a struct. To make an object with private
>> members, define an opaque struct. Put all the member definitions in one
>> translation unit, and define private members with the "static" modifier.
> 
> So far so good to use C constructs (a raw syntax, but effective).
> 
>> To implement inheritance, define a new struct with the base struct as
>> the first member.
> 
> Containment is not inheritance (even if using function pointers).

No, it's an implementation of inheritance. The user of these structures 
doesn't need to know they're implemented by inheritance.

>> Then, a cast (struct base *) will convert a derived
>> pointer to a base pointer.
> 
> OO concepts and static type casting are completely different concepts;
> one of the central OO characteristic is polymorphism.

...and polymorphism is acheived by static type casting. If I have a 
struct base * it may point to a struct derived1 or a struct derived2 or 
a plain struct base. I can call a struct base function on a struct 
derived1 by use of a cast.

>> Virtual functions can be implemented with function pointers.
> 
> Not really. (See casting above.)

I don't see how that refutes my statement.

>> There is nothing about the C language itself which prevents OO design.
> 
> Well, you can structure your code in some way to achieve information
> hiding, can associate data and functions in file packages as modules.
> That's good.
> 
> But to implement an OO design with real inheritance and polymorphism,
> and all that one would have to implement all the low level OO
> administration code (something like C++'s virtual function tables, for
> example) explicitly.

If the low level OO admin code is in a carefully-written library, which 
the end-user only has to use, this is fine. This is the case in GTK+.

> The essence is that you can of course write kind of OO even in some
> assembler, but the point of OO languages is to support the concept
> without bothering the programmer with the technical implementation
> internally necessary to support object orientation.

...which GTK+ achieves.

>> It's a little clunky, but it works and is readable and maintainable. I
>> don't understand what you have against this idea.
> 
> You can of course emulate some OO concepts by clunky constructs, and
> organize them in a sophisticated and relative readable way. Not that
> I'd call the outcome of an OO emulation in a low level language like C
> as "maintainable", though, rather extreemly error prone.

GTK+ is a success. Many programs are written using GTK+, and they seem 
to work and be maintainable. I find GTK+ easy to use, and naturally 
writes maintainable code.

I'm willing to admit that the internals of GTK+ could be messy, but 
that's not my problem; and GTK+ works well enough to support a large 
number of applications which use it.

I guess that GTK+ is an OO library in a C syntax, but to write generic 
OO in C is more difficult. It is more difficult to write your own class 
derived from GtkWidget than it would be in C++. But that is not to say 
that no OO possible in C, which was the assertion I was arguing against.

